WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.600
All right, friends.

00:04.600 --> 00:09.680
So with that, you have covered everything about the subqueries, all the different categories and types

00:09.680 --> 00:10.680
of the subqueries.

00:10.720 --> 00:13.560
And now we're going to do a quick recap about the subqueries.

00:13.560 --> 00:18.360
So as we learned subqueries is just simply a query inside another query.

00:18.360 --> 00:25.680
And we use the subqueries in order to break down complex queries into smaller, simpler, easy to manage

00:25.680 --> 00:26.360
pieces.

00:26.360 --> 00:29.760
That makes everything easier to develop and as well to read.

00:29.760 --> 00:33.920
And as we learned, there are like many different use cases for the subqueries.

00:33.920 --> 00:40.360
So we use subqueries in order to create temporary result sets to be used later from another query.

00:40.360 --> 00:46.400
And we learned that we can use the subqueries in order to prepare the data before joining the tables.

00:46.400 --> 00:52.360
And another very important use case for the subquery is that we can use it in order to filter our data

00:52.400 --> 00:56.440
using a dynamic and as well complex filter logics.

00:56.440 --> 01:01.960
And as we learned, we can go and use the correlated subqueries using the exist operator in order to

01:02.000 --> 01:06.400
check the existence of data and rows from another tables.

01:06.400 --> 01:11.560
And as well using the correlated subqueries help us to do row by row comparison.

01:11.560 --> 01:12.360
All right, my friends.

01:12.360 --> 01:16.720
So with that, we have covered an important technique on how to nest your queries in SQL.

01:16.760 --> 01:21.560
Now in the next step we're going to talk about one of the most famous techniques on how to do multi

01:21.560 --> 01:25.040
steps in SQL, the CTE common table expression.

01:25.040 --> 01:25.680
So let's go.
